WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.335 --> 00:00:01.410
(syringe squelching)

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.243
- Well, isn't that odd?

00:00:02.243 --> 00:00:03.076
- (laughing) Okay!

00:00:03.076 --> 00:00:04.560
We watched the medical dramas.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:05.800
We played the games.

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:08.540
But now it's time to take
a look at one of the most

00:00:12.184 --> 00:00:13.017
- Clear!

00:00:13.017 --> 00:00:15.384
(electricity buzzing)
(Homer grunts)

00:00:15.384 --> 00:00:17.024
(electricity buzzing)
(Homer grunts)

00:00:17.024 --> 00:00:18.104
- More.

00:00:18.104 --> 00:00:19.784
- Why are these shocking everybody?

00:00:19.784 --> 00:00:22.234
Even in the cartoons, they
take it from the medical dramas

00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:23.284
and they're shocking everybody.

00:00:23.284 --> 00:00:25.004
Chest compressions if
their heart has stopped

00:00:25.004 --> 00:00:25.837
and they're not breathing.

00:00:25.837 --> 00:00:28.209
And my man's going, "More. More."

00:00:28.209 --> 00:00:29.671
- Well, we can't fix his heart,

00:00:29.671 --> 00:00:32.724
but we can tell you
exactly how damaged it is.

00:00:32.724 --> 00:00:34.504
- What an age we live in.

00:00:34.504 --> 00:00:35.614
- It's classic in medicine

00:00:35.614 --> 00:00:37.074
where we don't have all the solutions

00:00:37.074 --> 00:00:38.631
and people right away started saying,

00:00:38.631 --> 00:00:41.351
"Oh, medicine sucks. Well,
how come we don't have this?

00:00:41.351 --> 00:00:42.911
"Maybe you're just a
doctor that doesn't know

00:00:42.911 --> 00:00:44.484
"what the cure to this problem is."

00:00:44.484 --> 00:00:46.864
No, the human body's very complex.

00:00:46.864 --> 00:00:48.064
And while we know a lot about it,

00:00:48.064 --> 00:00:49.604
there's still a lot more left to learn.

00:00:49.604 --> 00:00:52.124
- Homer, this is a new
body fat analysis test.

00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:55.683
I start you jiggling and measure
how long it takes to stop.

00:00:55.683 --> 00:00:56.894
(belly squelching)

00:00:56.894 --> 00:00:58.014
Caliper tests,

00:00:58.014 --> 00:01:00.654
weightless pool tests
for body fat percentage,

00:01:00.654 --> 00:01:03.077
punch in the guts test in "The Simpsons."

00:01:03.077 --> 00:01:04.184
- Mr. Simpson,

00:01:04.184 --> 00:01:07.194
I'm afraid you have a
crayon lodged in your brain.

00:01:07.194 --> 00:01:09.157
- There's a crayon in my brain?

00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:13.484
But I've had thousands of head X-rays.

00:01:13.484 --> 00:01:15.394
How come no one ever noticed it before?

00:01:15.394 --> 00:01:16.634
- I can answer that.

00:01:16.634 --> 00:01:19.094
You see, whenever I picked up an X-ray,

00:01:19.094 --> 00:01:21.034
I always hold it like this.

00:01:21.034 --> 00:01:23.274
My thumb must have covered up the crayon.

00:01:23.274 --> 00:01:24.894
- When you look at an X-ray,

00:01:24.894 --> 00:01:26.774
you have to look at many things,

00:01:26.774 --> 00:01:28.104
including the issue at hand.

00:01:28.104 --> 00:01:29.194
So if a person has shortness of breath,

00:01:29.194 --> 00:01:30.134
you wanna look at the lungs.

00:01:30.134 --> 00:01:33.114
You also wanna look at
the surrounding structures

00:01:33.114 --> 00:01:35.324
because the last thing you
wanna do is look at an X-ray

00:01:35.324 --> 00:01:36.844
to see if the patient has a pneumonia

00:01:36.844 --> 00:01:39.344
and missed the cancer that's
growing in their humerus,

00:01:39.344 --> 00:01:41.454
or on their rib, or on their clavicle.

00:01:41.454 --> 00:01:44.804
- This place seems even
nicer than the Mayo Clinic.

00:01:44.804 --> 00:01:46.613
- Mayo Clinic, eh?

00:01:46.613 --> 00:01:47.884
(gentle harp music)

00:01:47.884 --> 00:01:51.763
- Should we slice them diagonally
or just cut off the crust?

00:01:51.763 --> 00:01:52.704
- Aw!

00:01:52.704 --> 00:01:54.244
Why is it called the Mayo Clinic?

00:01:54.244 --> 00:01:56.114
It's gotta be because there was a doctor

00:01:56.114 --> 00:01:57.794
that started with the last name Mayo.

00:01:57.794 --> 00:01:59.634
It has nothing to do
with mayonnaise. (laughs)

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:02.374
- [Sam] Founders Charles and William Mayo.

00:02:02.374 --> 00:02:03.614
- Look at that!

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:09.402
(cat purring)

00:02:09.402 --> 00:02:10.995
Too wonderful.

00:02:10.995 --> 00:02:13.321
(dramatic music)
(car locks slamming)

00:02:13.321 --> 00:02:15.547
(car tires screeching)
(Dr. Hibbert laughing)

00:02:15.547 --> 00:02:16.716
- It's shot day!

00:02:16.716 --> 00:02:18.363
(children screaming)

00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:20.633
- There's actually a really
adorable video of a doctor

00:02:20.633 --> 00:02:23.413
who does this whole show
for kids with the needle

00:02:23.413 --> 00:02:24.773
to get them excited about it.

00:02:24.773 --> 00:02:27.393
And then fake injects himself
and then injects the child.

00:02:27.393 --> 00:02:28.573
And then they don't even feel the pain

00:02:28.573 --> 00:02:29.703
'cause there's so much going on.

00:02:29.703 --> 00:02:31.723
For kids who are able to reason somewhat,

00:02:31.723 --> 00:02:34.413
I try and talk to them
as if they're adults.

00:02:34.413 --> 00:02:35.413
I bargain with them.

00:02:35.413 --> 00:02:39.293
And I say, "I'm gonna give
you this really cool pillow."

00:02:39.293 --> 00:02:41.000
And they're like, "Why do we want a pillow

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:41.843
"that looks like you?"

00:02:41.843 --> 00:02:42.753
And I'm like, "True."

00:02:42.753 --> 00:02:44.713
And I get them something else

00:02:44.713 --> 00:02:46.973
that is furry and cute
and soft, like bear.

00:02:46.973 --> 00:02:48.943
Or I give them stickers
of their favorite shows.

00:02:48.943 --> 00:02:50.110
And they're like, "Oh, that's awesome.

00:02:50.110 --> 00:02:50.943
"What are we doing today?"

00:02:50.943 --> 00:02:52.980
I'm like, "Well, today your
gonna get a vaccine, a shot.

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:54.763
"Here's why," blah, blah, blah.

00:02:54.763 --> 00:02:56.040
They're like, "Okay, that sounds fair.

00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.600
"You gave me a sticker and a weird pillow.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:02:58.663
"So you must be nice."

00:02:58.663 --> 00:02:59.703
I give them a quick shot.

00:02:59.703 --> 00:03:01.633
I'm very gentle. We're very good with it.

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:02.903
My nurses are amazing.

00:03:02.903 --> 00:03:03.736
And then they leave happy.

00:03:03.736 --> 00:03:06.163
"Hibbert Moneymaking Organization."

00:03:06.163 --> 00:03:09.243
Is that what HMO stands
for this whole time?

00:03:09.243 --> 00:03:11.431
- I can provide every medical
service you'll ever need

00:03:11.431 --> 00:03:12.783
under one roof.

00:03:12.783 --> 00:03:15.153
- Rub a palm-full of this medicated salve

00:03:15.153 --> 00:03:17.343
into your scalp every half an hour.

00:03:17.343 --> 00:03:20.443
Keep him away from open
flames and pregnant women.

00:03:20.443 --> 00:03:23.333
- These should help those
fallen arches. Enjoy.

00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:25.783
- Hmm, throat looks a little red.

00:03:25.783 --> 00:03:26.990
I better spray it.

00:03:26.990 --> 00:03:30.303
- (laughing) Oh, my God. This
is my favorite skit ever.

00:03:30.303 --> 00:03:32.603
I think it has less to do with HMOs.

00:03:32.603 --> 00:03:35.423
But in general, the fact
that people in our society

00:03:35.423 --> 00:03:37.613
have grown up with this
theory inside their head,

00:03:37.613 --> 00:03:40.453
that they need to see a specialist
in order to stay healthy,

00:03:40.453 --> 00:03:41.833
and it's so not true.

00:03:41.833 --> 00:03:43.523
Because specialists oftentimes

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:45.493
deal with really complex cases,

00:03:45.493 --> 00:03:48.273
they treat things a little
bit more aggressively

00:03:48.273 --> 00:03:50.783
than your family medicine or
your primary care doctor would.

00:03:50.783 --> 00:03:52.673
Please don't think you
need to see a specialist

00:03:52.673 --> 00:03:54.873
for everything because,
A, that will happen.

00:03:54.873 --> 00:03:57.573
B, many things will go away on their own,

00:03:57.573 --> 00:03:59.353
and you don't need to
spend a whole ton of money

00:03:59.353 --> 00:04:01.053
to see a specialist in
order for that to happen.

00:04:01.053 --> 00:04:03.223
And, C, your primary care doctor's

00:04:03.223 --> 00:04:05.413
well versed in solving all these problems.

00:04:05.413 --> 00:04:07.163
And not only are we well
versed in solving them,

00:04:07.163 --> 00:04:10.003
we know you best 'cause we
have an existing relationship.

00:04:10.003 --> 00:04:12.833
- Now, Doctor, I want you
to test me for everything.

00:04:12.833 --> 00:04:14.816
Every disease on this chart.

00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:18.983
- Fine, we'll just start
by drawing some blood.

00:04:18.983 --> 00:04:19.963
(syringe squelching)

00:04:19.963 --> 00:04:20.859
Well, isn't that odd?

00:04:20.859 --> 00:04:23.443
- (laughing) Okay! I'm
gonna hit "Simpsons" hard

00:04:23.443 --> 00:04:24.783
with the medical inaccuracies.

00:04:24.783 --> 00:04:26.813
We do not stab patients with the syringe

00:04:26.813 --> 00:04:28.513
and have it come out the
other side of their arm,

00:04:28.513 --> 00:04:30.433
and be like, "Oh, I
guess it's not working."

00:04:30.433 --> 00:04:33.623
We do not just check
for a bunch of diseases.

00:04:33.623 --> 00:04:37.373
When you come in for a checkup,
part of that is figuring out

00:04:37.373 --> 00:04:39.243
what diseases we can actually screen for.

00:04:39.243 --> 00:04:40.813
There are some, like cancers.

00:04:40.813 --> 00:04:42.253
What diseases we can prevent.

00:04:42.253 --> 00:04:44.953
We could also look at what risks you face.

00:04:44.953 --> 00:04:47.973
So get this, a patient comes
in, doesn't have liver disease,

00:04:47.973 --> 00:04:50.353
but they talk to me honestly,
we have a good conversation

00:04:50.353 --> 00:04:51.673
and finds out they're drinking

00:04:51.673 --> 00:04:53.543
a bottle and a half of vodka a day.

00:04:53.543 --> 00:04:55.423
It's a lot, but some people do do this.

00:04:55.423 --> 00:04:57.303
We have a conversation about, A,

00:04:57.303 --> 00:04:59.743
what can we do to prevent them
from drinking and driving?

00:04:59.743 --> 00:05:03.183
What we can do for them,
preventing themselves causing harm

00:05:03.183 --> 00:05:05.253
in other ways to themselves,
when they are inebriated.

00:05:05.253 --> 00:05:06.163
How can we get them

00:05:06.163 --> 00:05:08.183
to cut down the amount
that they're drinking

00:05:08.183 --> 00:05:09.713
so they can stay healthier longer.

00:05:09.713 --> 00:05:12.173
Look at how much I've
affected their lifespan,

00:05:12.173 --> 00:05:13.533
the progression of disease,

00:05:13.533 --> 00:05:15.633
simply by having a good conversation.

00:05:15.633 --> 00:05:17.343
That's what you should
expect from a doctor

00:05:17.343 --> 00:05:18.553
when you go in for a physical.

00:05:18.553 --> 00:05:20.133
And if you get anything less than that,

00:05:20.133 --> 00:05:21.623
please look for a second opinion.

00:05:21.623 --> 00:05:23.533
- Mr. Burns, I'm afraid you are

00:05:23.533 --> 00:05:25.533
the sickest man in the United States.

00:05:25.533 --> 00:05:27.053
You have everything.

00:05:27.053 --> 00:05:28.433
- You mean I have pneumonia?
- Yes.

00:05:28.433 --> 00:05:29.943
- Juvenile diabetes?
- Yes.

00:05:29.943 --> 00:05:31.553
- Hysterical pregnancy?

00:05:31.553 --> 00:05:32.673
- A little bit. Yes.

00:05:32.673 --> 00:05:34.143
You also have several diseases

00:05:34.143 --> 00:05:35.443
that have just been discovered.

00:05:35.443 --> 00:05:37.263
- First of all, how does
he have juvenile diabetes?

00:05:37.263 --> 00:05:39.017
He's not of juvenile age.

00:05:39.017 --> 00:05:40.063
(text dinging)

00:05:40.063 --> 00:05:41.523
- This sounds like bad news.

00:05:41.523 --> 00:05:42.533
- Well, you'd think so,

00:05:42.533 --> 00:05:45.323
but all of your diseases
are in perfect balance.

00:05:45.323 --> 00:05:47.533
Here's the door to your body. You see?

00:05:47.533 --> 00:05:50.583
And these are oversized, novelty germs.

00:05:50.583 --> 00:05:52.523
That's influenza, that's bronchitis,

00:05:52.523 --> 00:05:53.883
and this cute little cuddle bug

00:05:53.883 --> 00:05:55.613
is pancreatic cancer. (chuckles)

00:05:55.613 --> 00:05:56.743
Here's what happens

00:05:56.743 --> 00:05:59.276
when they all try to get
through the door at once.

00:06:00.956 --> 00:06:03.753
(whooping) Move it, chowder head!

00:06:03.753 --> 00:06:05.696
We call it Three Stooges Syndrome.

00:06:06.663 --> 00:06:07.496
- No.

00:06:07.496 --> 00:06:09.723
If you have a lot of diseases,

00:06:09.723 --> 00:06:12.563
it's not better than having less diseases.

00:06:12.563 --> 00:06:14.233
Also, if you're a good doctor

00:06:14.233 --> 00:06:15.973
and a lot of tests come up positive,

00:06:15.973 --> 00:06:18.913
you need to start looking for a cause

00:06:18.913 --> 00:06:21.803
that perhaps ties in all
of these diseases together.

00:06:21.803 --> 00:06:23.700
I don't just say, "Hey, you
got a bunch of diseases.

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:25.113
"Deuces."

00:06:25.113 --> 00:06:27.463
- There's a crazy man
with a scalpel in ER!

00:06:27.463 --> 00:06:29.934
He's demanding to see a quack!

00:06:29.934 --> 00:06:32.858
(dramatic music)

00:06:32.858 --> 00:06:34.863
(Abe yelling)

00:06:34.863 --> 00:06:35.913
- Hi, everybody.

00:06:35.913 --> 00:06:38.987
Now, tell Dr. Nick where is the trouble?

00:06:38.987 --> 00:06:41.513
- I'm itchy! I got ants in my pants!

00:06:41.513 --> 00:06:44.423
I'm discombobulated! Get me a calmative!

00:06:44.423 --> 00:06:45.653
- Slow down, sir!

00:06:45.653 --> 00:06:48.116
You're going to give
yourself skin failure.

00:06:48.116 --> 00:06:49.603
- What is skin fail?

00:06:49.603 --> 00:06:51.733
- I think skin failure is
when your skin just gives up

00:06:51.733 --> 00:06:52.963
and starts sloughing off.

00:06:52.963 --> 00:06:54.395
Steven Johnson Syndrome.

00:06:54.395 --> 00:06:56.713
(text tinkling)

00:06:56.713 --> 00:06:58.603
- [Instructor] Insert the
retractor and crank it

00:06:58.603 --> 00:07:01.300
until the ribs swing open,
like a rusty drawbridge.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.823
(body squelching)
Ah! No, blood!

00:07:03.823 --> 00:07:05.243
- Silly, but truth to it.

00:07:05.243 --> 00:07:08.233
We do watch videos on
procedures all the time.

00:07:08.233 --> 00:07:09.233
And that's a good thing!

00:07:09.233 --> 00:07:11.203
You want your doctor up to date

00:07:11.203 --> 00:07:13.043
and seeing the procedure
before they do it.

00:07:13.043 --> 00:07:14.963
Even if they've done it multiple times,

00:07:14.963 --> 00:07:17.703
that little refresher
goes a long, long way.

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:19.493
And you know where we a lot of it?

00:07:19.493 --> 00:07:20.326
YouTube.

00:07:20.326 --> 00:07:21.933
Your doctor watches YouTube

00:07:21.933 --> 00:07:23.793
before they perform surgeries on you.

00:07:23.793 --> 00:07:24.953
That's the truth.

00:07:24.953 --> 00:07:27.773
- [Dr. Nick] These gloves came
free with my toilet brush.

00:07:27.773 --> 00:07:29.753
- Usually. you have someone
help you with the gloves,

00:07:29.753 --> 00:07:30.993
have the package open

00:07:30.993 --> 00:07:33.413
because remember the inside
of the package is sterile,

00:07:33.413 --> 00:07:34.583
but the outside is not.

00:07:34.583 --> 00:07:37.613
So there's a specific way
you have to put the gloves on

00:07:37.613 --> 00:07:39.644
to make sure that you're keeping sterile.

00:07:39.644 --> 00:07:43.030
(Homer breathing deeply)
(air hissing)

00:07:43.030 --> 00:07:44.193
- [Dr. Nick] What the hell is that?

00:07:44.193 --> 00:07:45.963
- That's something you don't
wanna hear from your surgeon.

00:07:45.963 --> 00:07:47.533
That and, "Oops!"

00:07:47.533 --> 00:07:50.983
- I know I'm supposed to cut
something, but what and where?

00:07:50.983 --> 00:07:53.543
- The mask is not part
of the sterile field.

00:07:53.543 --> 00:07:54.923
So if you make contact with your mask,

00:07:54.923 --> 00:07:57.493
right away, you have to leave,
change gloves, re-scrub in,

00:07:57.493 --> 00:07:59.103
and then do the whole
process all over again.

00:07:59.103 --> 00:08:01.033
So my man is not sterile no more!

00:08:01.033 --> 00:08:02.953
- Dr. Nick Riviera!

00:08:02.953 --> 00:08:04.033
Remember me?

00:08:04.033 --> 00:08:07.363
- Well, if it isn't my
old friend, Mr. McGreg!

00:08:07.363 --> 00:08:10.233
With a leg for an arm
and an arm for a leg.

00:08:10.233 --> 00:08:12.063
- The only question I have
after watching that is

00:08:12.063 --> 00:08:13.653
what is that doctor's specialty?

00:08:13.653 --> 00:08:15.390
Dr. Nick, what do you do?

00:08:15.390 --> 00:08:17.023
"Simpsons," all in all,

00:08:17.023 --> 00:08:19.013
does a great job of teaching you medicine.

00:08:19.013 --> 00:08:20.123
Albeit satirically,

00:08:20.123 --> 00:08:22.703
but as you've seen with The
Onion episodes in the past,

00:08:27.444 --> 00:08:30.544
and my "Saturday Night
Live" medical skit reviews.

00:08:30.544 --> 00:08:32.824
Which one are you clicking?
Which one are you enjoying?

00:08:32.824 --> 00:08:35.510
And as always, stay happy and healthy.

00:08:35.510 --> 00:08:38.760
(gentle upbeat music)

